            'expected_system_design' => 'At a high level, the expected system design follows this flow: Camera frame -> fingertip tracking -> traced-symbol recognition -> expression evaluation and on-screen result. Each stage should be treated as a distinct, testable module: the input-handling stage is responsible only for acquiring and preprocessing raw input, the processing stage is responsible only for the core AI/robotics logic described above, and the output stage is responsible only for presenting or acting on the result. This separation makes the system considerably easier to debug and to extend if the trainee chooses to add features beyond the base scope.',
            'team_contribution_statement' => 'Suggested split for a 2-3 person team: one member owns the core AI/vision logic; one owns the input pipeline and output/UI presentation; if a third member is available, they own testing, documentation, and the demo script.',
            'success_criteria' => 'This project should be considered successfully complete when: the core Computer Vision capability works reliably on live input (not just a single prepared example); the system\'s behavior is predictable enough to demo confidently without manual workarounds; and the final report/documentation clearly explains what was built, how it was tested, and any known limitations — a project that works but is undocumented, or is documented but does not actually run live, are both considered incomplete.',
            'technology_stack___tools' => 'Suggested tools for this specific project: OpenCV and Python as the core toolkit, plus any lightweight library matching the specific sub-task. Keep the toolset as small as possible — adding a library only when the simpler option genuinely cannot do the job avoids unnecessary setup time and dependency issues during a short training period.',
            'anticipated_challenges___risk_mitigation' => 'Likely technical challenges for a Computer Vision project at beginner level include: variability in real-world input compared to any example/test data used during development (lighting, noise, phrasing, or motion variation); balancing response speed against accuracy, especially if the system needs to feel responsive in a live demo; and, for a purely software project, making sure the demo environment (camera angle, lighting, microphone placement) is controlled well enough on presentation day to reproduce development-time results. Planning a small buffer of extra time for demo-environment rehearsal is strongly recommended.',
            'ethical___safety_considerations' => 'Standard data-handling care applies: do not expose any personally identifiable data captured during testing/demos beyond the training team.',
            'planned_implementation_approach__to_be_expanded_with_real_work_' => 'A reasonable breakdown for this project is three stages, each independently testable: (1) Input handling / data capture module — get raw input flowing reliably before adding any intelligence; (2) Core AI/vision processing module — implement and validate the core Computer Vision logic in isolation, ideally with a small set of saved test inputs before wiring it to live input; (3) Output / result-presentation module — connect the validated logic to its final output/action and polish the end-to-end experience. Building and testing each stage separately, in this order, avoids the common failure mode of debugging a fully connected system with no idea which stage is at fault.',
            'test_plan__results_added_once_testing_is_performed_' => 'A minimal but meaningful test plan for AI Calculator Using Hand Gestures should include: a "normal case" test (typical, expected input, confirming the system produces a sensible result); an "edge case" test (partial/ambiguous input — e.g. poor lighting for a vision project, or background noise for an audio project — confirming the system fails gracefully rather than crashing); and a "repeatability" test (running the same input multiple times to confirm the result is consistent). Document expected behavior for each case before implementation begins, then fill in actual behavior once testing is performed during the Documentation stage.',
            'starter_reference_list__expand_as_more_sources_are_used_' => 'Recommended starting references for this project: OpenCV official documentation, and the official documentation of any additional library used.',
            'appendix_a' => 'Supporting Materials Index:
Suggested appendix contents for this project: (1) screenshots or short screen recordings of the system running on at least two different inputs (a normal case and an edge case); (2) the full source code listing or a link to the project\'s GitHub repository; (3) any sample data used for testing (e.g. sample images, a small labeled dataset, or a list of test commands used); (4) a brief note on the exact library versions used, to aid reproducibility.
