        'id'            => 16,
        'title'         => 'Smart Security Camera',
        'category'      => 'software',
        'level'         => 'Beginner',
        'skills'        => '',
        'display_order' => 16,
        'sections'      => [
            'abstract' => 'Smart Security Camera is a intermediate-level project that watches a video feed and raises an alert when unexpected motion or a person is detected outside allowed hours. The project addresses a practical need: reviewing hours of raw camera footage manually to find one relevant event is inefficient and error-prone. It is implemented by combining motion detection with person detection, and triggering a logged alert only when both conditions match the configured rule. The expected outcome is a working, demonstrable system that clearly shows the target AI or robotics capability in action, suitable for evaluation against the program\'s standard assessment criteria.',
            'introduction_background' => 'Computer-vision and AI software projects at this level typically move from a working perception step (detecting or classifying something in an image or video stream) to a usable decision or output layer built on top of it. The value of such a system is judged less by algorithmic novelty and more by whether the pipeline is reliable enough to demonstrate convincingly end-to-end. In the specific context of this project, the trainee will work on a system that watches a video feed and raises an alert when unexpected motion or a person is detected outside allowed hours. This places the project squarely within the software/AI track of the training program, using AI Vision as its primary technical foundation.',
            'problem_definition' => 'The core problem this project addresses is that reviewing hours of raw camera footage manually to find one relevant event is inefficient and error-prone. Left unaddressed, this gap means the relevant task continues to depend on manual effort or goes unaddressed altogether. The specific objectives of this project are: (1) implement a working smart security camera pipeline using AI Vision; (2) demonstrate the pipeline reliably on realistic input, not just a single cherry-picked example; (3) document the system\'s behavior, including its known limitations, clearly enough for an evaluator to understand what was and was not achieved.',
            'objectives_scope' => 'In scope: building and testing the core smart security camera pipeline described in this proposal; demonstrating it live or via recorded evidence during evaluation; documenting the approach, implementation, and results in the required report format. Out of scope: large-scale production deployment, extensive dataset collection beyond what is reasonably available for a training-length project, and features not directly tied to the core capability described above unless time permits as a stretch goal.',
            'related_work' => 'Prior approaches to this class of problem generally fall into two families: classical image-processing pipelines (color thresholding, edge/contour detection, handcrafted feature matching) which are fast and easy to reason about but fragile under lighting or viewpoint changes, and learned models (CNN-based classifiers/detectors, or pretrained landmark/embedding models) which generalize better but require a labeled dataset or a suitable pretrained model to start from. Most practical student-level systems in this space combine both: a learned model for the core perception step, wrapped in simpler classical logic for triggering, thresholds, and output handling. For this project specifically, the closest relevant pattern is the use of AI Vision to achieve the described behavior, and the trainee is expected to briefly survey at least one or two existing open-source implementations of a similar capability before finalizing their own design, to avoid reinventing solved sub-problems unnecessarily.',
            'methodology' => 'The proposed approach is combining motion detection with person detection, and triggering a logged alert only when both conditions match the configured rule. This was chosen over more complex alternatives because it offers a reasonable balance between implementation effort and reliability within the training timeframe, while still meaningfully exercising AI Vision. The trainee should validate each stage of the pipeline independently before integrating the full system, so that failures can be isolated to a specific stage rather than debugged end-to-end.',
            'expected_system_design' => 'At a high level, the expected system design follows this flow: Video frame -> motion detection + person detection -> rule match (time window, both conditions) -> logged alert. Each stage should be treated as a distinct, testable module: the input-handling stage is responsible only for acquiring and preprocessing raw input, the processing stage is responsible only for the core AI/robotics logic described above, and the output stage is responsible only for presenting or acting on the result. This separation makes the system considerably easier to debug and to extend if the trainee chooses to add features beyond the base scope.',
            'team_contribution_statement' => 'Suggested split for a 2-3 person team: one member owns the core AI/vision logic; one owns the input pipeline and output/UI presentation; if a third member is available, they own testing, documentation, and the demo script.',
            'success_criteria' => 'This project should be considered successfully complete when: the core AI Vision capability works reliably on live input (not just a single prepared example); the system\'s behavior is predictable enough to demo confidently without manual workarounds; and the final report/documentation clearly explains what was built, how it was tested, and any known limitations — a project that works but is undocumented, or is documented but does not actually run live, are both considered incomplete.',
            'technology_stack___tools' => 'Suggested tools for this specific project: OpenCV plus a pretrained detection/classification model appropriate to the task. Keep the toolset as small as possible — adding a library only when the simpler option genuinely cannot do the job avoids unnecessary setup time and dependency issues during a short training period.',
            'anticipated_challenges___risk_mitigation' => 'Likely technical challenges for a AI Vision project at intermediate level include: variability in real-world input compared to any example/test data used during development (lighting, noise, phrasing, or motion variation); balancing response speed against accuracy, especially if the system needs to feel responsive in a live demo; and, for a purely software project, making sure the demo environment (camera angle, lighting, microphone placement) is controlled well enough on presentation day to reproduce development-time results. Planning a small buffer of extra time for demo-environment rehearsal is strongly recommended.',
            'ethical___safety_considerations' => 'Standard data-handling care applies: do not expose any personally identifiable data captured during testing/demos beyond the training team.',
            'planned_implementation_approach__to_be_expanded_with_real_work_' => 'A reasonable breakdown for this project is three stages, each independently testable: (1) Input handling / data capture module — get raw input flowing reliably before adding any intelligence; (2) Core AI/vision processing module — implement and validate the core AI Vision logic in isolation, ideally with a small set of saved test inputs before wiring it to live input; (3) Output / result-presentation module — connect the validated logic to its final output/action and polish the end-to-end experience. Building and testing each stage separately, in this order, avoids the common failure mode of debugging a fully connected system with no idea which stage is at fault.',
            'test_plan__results_added_once_testing_is_performed_' => 'A minimal but meaningful test plan for Smart Security Camera should include: a "normal case" test (typical, expected input, confirming the system produces a sensible result); an "edge case" test (partial/ambiguous input — e.g. poor lighting for a vision project, or background noise for an audio project — confirming the system fails gracefully rather than crashing); and a "repeatability" test (running the same input multiple times to confirm the result is consistent). Document expected behavior for each case before implementation begins, then fill in actual behavior once testing is performed during the Documentation stage.',
            'starter_reference_list__expand_as_more_sources_are_used_' => 'Recommended starting references for this project: OpenCV official documentation, and the official documentation of any additional library used.',
            'appendix_a' => 'Supporting Materials Index:
Suggested appendix contents for this project: (1) screenshots or short screen recordings of the system running on at least two different inputs (a normal case and an edge case); (2) the full source code listing or a link to the project\'s GitHub repository; (3) any sample data used for testing (e.g. sample images, a small labeled dataset, or a list of test commands used); (4) a brief note on the exact library versions used, to aid reproducibility.
