            'anticipated_challenges___risk_mitigation' => 'Likely technical challenges for a Navigation + Speech project at intermediate level include: variability in real-world input compared to any example/test data used during development (lighting, noise, phrasing, or motion variation); balancing response speed against accuracy, especially if the system needs to feel responsive in a live demo; and, for a robot-based project, the added complexity of physical timing and safe operating range on top of the software logic itself. Planning a small buffer of extra time for demo-environment rehearsal is strongly recommended.',
            'ethical___safety_considerations' => 'Standard data-handling care applies: do not expose any personally identifiable data captured during testing/demos beyond the training team. Physical safety: always test movement/behavior on a clear, flat surface with a teammate ready to intervene, and never leave the robot unattended while powered and mobile.',
            'planned_implementation_approach__to_be_expanded_with_real_work_' => 'A reasonable breakdown for this project is three stages, each independently testable: (1) Perception module (camera/sensor/microphone input) — get raw input flowing reliably before adding any intelligence; (2) Decision logic module (mapping perception to an action) — implement and validate the core Navigation + Speech logic in isolation, ideally with a small set of saved test inputs before wiring it to live input; (3) Robot action module (motion/speech/gesture execution) — connect the validated logic to its final output/action and polish the end-to-end experience. Building and testing each stage separately, in this order, avoids the common failure mode of debugging a fully connected system with no idea which stage is at fault.',
            'test_plan__results_added_once_testing_is_performed_' => 'A minimal but meaningful test plan for Museum Tour Guide should include: a "normal case" test (typical, expected input, confirming the system produces a sensible result); an "edge case" test (partial/ambiguous input — e.g. poor lighting for a vision project, or background noise for an audio project — confirming the system fails gracefully rather than crashing); and a "repeatability" test (running the same input multiple times to confirm the result is consistent). Document expected behavior for each case before implementation begins, then fill in actual behavior once testing is performed during the Documentation stage.',
            'starter_reference_list__expand_as_more_sources_are_used_' => 'Recommended starting references for this project: OpenCV official documentation, and the official documentation of any additional library used.',
            'appendix_a' => 'Supporting Materials Index:
Suggested appendix contents for this project: (1) screenshots or short screen recordings of the system running on at least two different inputs (a normal case and an edge case); (2) the full source code listing or a link to the project\'s GitHub repository; (3) any sample data used for testing (e.g. sample images, a small labeled dataset, or a list of test commands used); (4) a short video clip of the robot performing the behavior, since a physical demo cannot always be reproduced live for every reader of the report.
