    [
        'id'            => 35,
        'title'         => 'Smart Reception Robot',
        'category'      => 'software',
        'level'         => 'Beginner',
        'skills'        => '',
        'display_order' => 35,
        'sections'      => [
            'abstract' => 'Smart Reception Robot is a advanced-level project that has Yanshee recognize a known visitor by face and greet them by name, or ask an unknown visitor for their purpose. The project addresses a practical need: a reception desk repeats the same simple interactions constantly, and a robot can absorb the routine, predictable cases. It is implemented by combining face recognition (enrolled versus unknown) with a small dialogue script and text-to-speech/motion responses per case. The expected outcome is a working, demonstrable system that clearly shows the target AI or robotics capability in action, suitable for evaluation against the program\'s standard assessment criteria.',
            'introduction_background' => 'Yanshee-based training projects sit at the intersection of embedded motion control and AI perception: the robot\'s own SDK/Blockly layer already provides reliable low-level walk/pose/gesture primitives, so the interesting engineering work is in the layer above it — deciding, from sensor or camera input, which primitive to trigger and when. In the specific context of this project, the trainee will work on a system that has Yanshee recognize a known visitor by face and greet them by name, or ask an unknown visitor for their purpose. This places the project squarely within the Yanshee robotics track of the training program, using AI + Face Recognition as its primary technical foundation.',
            'problem_definition' => 'The core problem this project addresses is that a reception desk repeats the same simple interactions constantly, and a robot can absorb the routine, predictable cases. Left unaddressed, this gap means the relevant task continues to depend on manual effort or goes unaddressed altogether. The specific objectives of this project are: (1) implement a working smart reception robot pipeline using AI + Face Recognition; (2) demonstrate the pipeline reliably on realistic input, not just a single cherry-picked example; (3) document the system\'s behavior, including its known limitations, clearly enough for an evaluator to understand what was and was not achieved.',
            'objectives_scope' => 'In scope: building and testing the core smart reception robot pipeline described in this proposal; demonstrating it live or via recorded evidence during evaluation; documenting the approach, implementation, and results in the required report format. Out of scope: large-scale production deployment, extensive dataset collection beyond what is reasonably available for a training-length project, and features not directly tied to the core capability described above unless time permits as a stretch goal.',
            'related_work' => 'Humanoid social-robotics projects at this scale typically follow a perceive-decide-act loop: a perception step (camera, distance sensor, or microphone) feeds a small decision layer (rule-based matching or simple state machine) which then triggers one of the robot\'s built-in motion/speech primitives. The main engineering risk is not the AI perception step itself but robust integration with the physical platform — timing, balance, and safe operating range. For this project specifically, the closest relevant pattern is the use of AI + Face Recognition to achieve the described behavior, and the trainee is expected to briefly survey at least one or two existing open-source implementations of a similar capability before finalizing their own design, to avoid reinventing solved sub-problems unnecessarily.',
            'methodology' => 'The proposed approach is combining face recognition (enrolled versus unknown) with a small dialogue script and text-to-speech/motion responses per case. This was chosen over more complex alternatives because it offers a reasonable balance between implementation effort and reliability within the training timeframe, while still meaningfully exercising AI + Face Recognition. The trainee should validate each stage of the pipeline independently before integrating the full system, so that failures can be isolated to a specific stage rather than debugged end-to-end.',
            'expected_system_design' => 'At a high level, the expected system design follows this flow: Camera frame -> face recognition (known/unknown) -> branch dialogue script -> speech + motion response. Each stage should be treated as a distinct, testable module: the input-handling stage is responsible only for acquiring and preprocessing raw input, the processing stage is responsible only for the core AI/robotics logic described above, and the output stage is responsible only for presenting or acting on the result. This separation makes the system considerably easier to debug and to extend if the trainee chooses to add features beyond the base scope.',
            'team_contribution_statement' => 'Suggested split for a 2-3 person team: one member owns the perception/AI logic; one owns the robot integration and motion/speech behavior; if a third member is available, they own testing, safety checks, and documentation.',
            'success_criteria' => 'This project should be considered successfully complete when: the core AI + Face Recognition capability works reliably on live input (not just a single prepared example); the system\'s behavior is predictable enough to demo confidently without manual workarounds; and the final report/documentation clearly explains what was built, how it was tested, and any known limitations — a project that works but is undocumented, or is documented but does not actually run live, are both considered incomplete.',
            'technology_stack___tools' => 'Suggested tools for this specific project: OpenCV, the face_recognition/dlib library (or a lightweight embedding model), plus a database for enrolled records. Keep the toolset as small as possible — adding a library only when the simpler option genuinely cannot do the job avoids unnecessary setup time and dependency issues during a short training period.',
            'anticipated_challenges___risk_mitigation' => 'Likely technical challenges for a AI + Face Recognition project at advanced level include: variability in real-world input compared to any example/test data used during development (lighting, noise, phrasing, or motion variation); balancing response speed against accuracy, especially if the system needs to feel responsive in a live demo; and, for a robot-based project, the added complexity of physical timing and safe operating range on top of the software logic itself. Planning a small buffer of extra time for demo-environment rehearsal is strongly recommended.',
            'ethical___safety_considerations' => 'Obtain clear consent before enrolling anyone\'s face, store biometric data securely, and provide a way to remove a person\'s data on request. Physical safety: always test movement/behavior on a clear, flat surface with a teammate ready to intervene, and never leave the robot unattended while powered and mobile.',
            'planned_implementation_approach__to_be_expanded_with_real_work_' => 'A reasonable breakdown for this project is three stages, each independently testable: (1) Perception module (camera/sensor/microphone input) — get raw input flowing reliably before adding any intelligence; (2) Decision logic module (mapping perception to an action) — implement and validate the core AI + Face Recognition logic in isolation, ideally with a small set of saved test inputs before wiring it to live input; (3) Robot action module (motion/speech/gesture execution) — connect the validated logic to its final output/action and polish the end-to-end experience. Building and testing each stage separately, in this order, avoids the common failure mode of debugging a fully connected system with no idea which stage is at fault.',
            'test_plan__results_added_once_testing_is_performed_' => 'A minimal but meaningful test plan for Smart Reception Robot should include: a "normal case" test (typical, expected input, confirming the system produces a sensible result); an "edge case" test (partial/ambiguous input — e.g. poor lighting for a vision project, or background noise for an audio project — confirming the system fails gracefully rather than crashing); and a "repeatability" test (running the same input multiple times to confirm the result is consistent). Document expected behavior for each case before implementation begins, then fill in actual behavior once testing is performed during the Documentation stage.',
            'starter_reference_list__expand_as_more_sources_are_used_' => 'Recommended starting references for this project: The face_recognition/dlib library documentation, and OpenCV\'s face-detection tutorials.',
            'appendix_a' => 'Supporting Materials Index:
Suggested appendix contents for this project: (1) screenshots or short screen recordings of the system running on at least two different inputs (a normal case and an edge case); (2) the full source code listing or a link to the project\'s GitHub repository; (3) any sample data used for testing (e.g. sample images, a small labeled dataset, or a list of test commands used); (4) a short video clip of the robot performing the behavior, since a physical demo cannot always be reproduced live for every reader of the report.
