            'expected_system_design' => 'At a high level, the expected system design follows this flow: Scheduled/triggered reminder -> dialogue script selection -> spoken check-in + gesture output. Each stage should be treated as a distinct, testable module: the input-handling stage is responsible only for acquiring and preprocessing raw input, the processing stage is responsible only for the core AI/robotics logic described above, and the output stage is responsible only for presenting or acting on the result. This separation makes the system considerably easier to debug and to extend if the trainee chooses to add features beyond the base scope.',
            'team_contribution_statement' => 'Suggested split for a 2-3 person team: one member owns the perception/AI logic; one owns the robot integration and motion/speech behavior; if a third member is available, they own testing, safety checks, and documentation.',
            'success_criteria' => 'This project should be considered successfully complete when: the core Human Interaction capability works reliably on live input (not just a single prepared example); the system\'s behavior is predictable enough to demo confidently without manual workarounds; and the final report/documentation clearly explains what was built, how it was tested, and any known limitations — a project that works but is undocumented, or is documented but does not actually run live, are both considered incomplete.',
            'technology_stack___tools' => 'Suggested tools for this specific project: OpenCV and Python as the core toolkit, plus any lightweight library matching the specific sub-task. Keep the toolset as small as possible — adding a library only when the simpler option genuinely cannot do the job avoids unnecessary setup time and dependency issues during a short training period.',
            'anticipated_challenges___risk_mitigation' => 'Likely technical challenges for a Human Interaction project at advanced level include: variability in real-world input compared to any example/test data used during development (lighting, noise, phrasing, or motion variation); balancing response speed against accuracy, especially if the system needs to feel responsive in a live demo; and, for a robot-based project, the added complexity of physical timing and safe operating range on top of the software logic itself. Planning a small buffer of extra time for demo-environment rehearsal is strongly recommended.',
            'ethical___safety_considerations' => 'Standard data-handling care applies: do not expose any personally identifiable data captured during testing/demos beyond the training team. Physical safety: always test movement/behavior on a clear, flat surface with a teammate ready to intervene, and never leave the robot unattended while powered and mobile.',
            'planned_implementation_approach__to_be_expanded_with_real_work_' => 'A reasonable breakdown for this project is three stages, each independently testable: (1) Perception module (camera/sensor/microphone input) — get raw input flowing reliably before adding any intelligence; (2) Decision logic module (mapping perception to an action) — implement and validate the core Human Interaction logic in isolation, ideally with a small set of saved test inputs before wiring it to live input; (3) Robot action module (motion/speech/gesture execution) — connect the validated logic to its final output/action and polish the end-to-end experience. Building and testing each stage separately, in this order, avoids the common failure mode of debugging a fully connected system with no idea which stage is at fault.',
            'test_plan__results_added_once_testing_is_performed_' => 'A minimal but meaningful test plan for Elderly Care Assistant should include: a "normal case" test (typical, expected input, confirming the system produces a sensible result); an "edge case" test (partial/ambiguous input — e.g. poor lighting for a vision project, or background noise for an audio project — confirming the system fails gracefully rather than crashing); and a "repeatability" test (running the same input multiple times to confirm the result is consistent). Document expected behavior for each case before implementation begins, then fill in actual behavior once testing is performed during the Documentation stage.',
            'starter_reference_list__expand_as_more_sources_are_used_' => 'Recommended starting references for this project: OpenCV official documentation, and the official documentation of any additional library used.',
            'appendix_a' => 'Supporting Materials Index:
Suggested appendix contents for this project: (1) screenshots or short screen recordings of the system running on at least two different inputs (a normal case and an edge case); (2) the full source code listing or a link to the project\'s GitHub repository; (3) any sample data used for testing (e.g. sample images, a small labeled dataset, or a list of test commands used); (4) a short video clip of the robot performing the behavior, since a physical demo cannot always be reproduced live for every reader of the report.
